KNOWNBUG
main.i
--no-div-by-zero-check --force-malloc-success --force-realloc-success --state-hashing --add-symex-value-sets --no-align-check --k-step 2 --floatbv --unlimited-k-steps --no-vla-size-check --64  --no-por --context-bound 3 --no-pointer-check --no-bounds-check --unsigned-overflow-check --no-assertions --incremental-bmc
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
This test's VERIFICATION FAILED was an artefact of a false positive in the
unsigned shl overflow check: overflow_arith() derived signedness from either
operand, so the SHA-512 table constants `(N_UL << 32) + M_UL` (main.i:3443+)
took the signed path and were reported as overflowing for any N >= 2^31, even
though every result is representable in u64.

With that fixed, symex gets further into the file and hits a separate,
pre-existing defect:

  terminate called after throwing an instance of 'array_type2t::dyn_sized_array_excp'
    what():  Sizeof nondeterministically sized array encountered

That crash is independent of the overflow encoding -- it reproduces with
--unsigned-overflow-check removed entirely. It is fixed separately, by
routing __builtin_object_size() on a dynamically sized array into the
"unknown size" answer this function already computes for unidentifiable
objects (PR #6687).

Fixing it is not sufficient to restore this test, however. With both
changes in place the run no longer aborts, but it does not terminate
either: --incremental-bmc --unlimited-k-steps over this file was still
unwinding after 40 minutes and 10 GB. The false positive had been
supplying an early exit, and without it there is no evidence this test
ever reached a verdict on its own merits.

Restoring it to CORE therefore needs a bound that makes it terminate --
most likely a --k-step ceiling in place of --unlimited-k-steps, chosen so
the expected VERIFICATION FAILED is actually reachable. That is a
decision about what this test is meant to check, which is why it is left
to whoever owns it rather than guessed at here.
